      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis is a classic performance engineering scenario often referred to as the difference between **warm throughput** (or cached throughput) and **cold throughput** (or fresh inference throughput).\n\nHere is a detailed analysis of the causes, methods to isolate valid metrics, and the necessary data points for publishing a credible benchmark.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Plausible Causes for the Discrepancy\n\nThe high tokens-per-second (TPS) on repeated prompts versus low TPS on unique prompts is almost certainly due to **KV-Cache Reuse** and **Prefill vs. Decode Dynamics**.\n\n#### A. KV-Cache Reuse (The Primary Cause)\nModern LLM inference engines (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, etc.) use a **Key-Value (KV) Cache** to store the attention states of previous tokens.\n*   **Repeated Prompts:** If the prompt is identical, the engine can detect that the KV cache for that prompt already exists in memory. It skips the **Prefill** phase (computing the KV for the input tokens) and jumps directly to the **Decode** phase (generating the next token). Since Prefill is computationally expensive (quadratic complexity with sequence length), skipping it drastically increases apparent TPS.\n*   **Unique Prompts:** The engine must perform a full **Prefill** for every new prompt. This involves matrix multiplications for all input tokens, which is much slower per token than the autoregressive decode step.\n\n#### B. Batch Size and Throughput Aggregation\n*   **Repeated Prompts:** If the benchmark sends the same prompt repeatedly, the engine may batch these requests together. If the system is optimized for high batch sizes, the GPU utilization is maximized, leading to high *aggregate* TPS.\n*   **Unique Prompts:** If unique prompts are sent sequentially or with low concurrency, the GPU may be underutilized during the Prefill phase, leading to lower observed TPS.\n\n#### C. Memory Bandwidth vs. Compute Bound\n*   **Decode Phase (Repeated):** Once the prompt is cached, generation is **memory-bandwidth bound**. The model weights are loaded from HBM (High Bandwidth Memory) for each token. This is relatively fast.\n*   **Prefill Phase (Unique):** This is **compute-bound**. It requires heavy FLOPs (Floating Point Operations) to process the input sequence. If the GPU is not fully saturated or if the sequence length is",
