      "reasoning_delta_count": 0,
      "request_ended_epoch_s": 1787859877.1366513,
      "request_id": "bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-06-architecture-tradeoff",
      "request_started_epoch_s": 1787859850.7545052,
      "response_id_first": null,
      "response_id_last": null,
      "response_x_request_id": null,
      "sha256": "0d3587ccc366d4ee3859077dcd3f7852031cd7640b5aa6627ef8b5a21dd31a20",
      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its own GPU, with a load balancer/router in front.\n\nI\u2019ll walk through the dimensions you asked about, then give a decision guide.\n\n1) Latency\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Time-to-first-token (TTFT): Often lower for large models because the forward pass is split across GPUs; each GPU does a fraction of the matmuls, and the result is combined via all-reduce. If interconnect is fast (NVLink), the added communication overhead is modest.\n  - Inter-token latency (decode): Each token still requires an all-reduce (or similar) across GPUs per layer. With NVLink this is usually fine; over PCIe it can add noticeable per-token latency.\n  - Tail latency: Can be sensitive to stragglers \u2014 one slow GPU or a PCIe hiccup stalls the whole instance.\n- 4 independent 1-GPU replicas\n  - TTFT: Each replica runs the full model on one GPU. If the model fits comfortably in one GPU\u2019s memory and compute, TTFT can be very low and predictable. If the model is near the edge of what one GPU can handle, TTFT may be higher than TP.\n  - Inter-token latency: No cross-GPU communication per token; each replica is self-contained. Latency is dominated by single-GPU compute and memory bandwidth.\n  - Tail latency: More isolated \u2014 a slow request on one replica doesn\u2019t affect others. However, if one GPU is thermally throttling or has a bad PCIe link, only that replica is affected.\n\nVerdict:\n- If the model is large enough that one GPU is compute/memory-bound, TP usually gives lower TTFT and can maintain good inter-token latency if you have NVLink.\n- If the model fits well on one GPU and you have PCIe-only interconnect, replicas often give more predictable, lower tail latency because there\u2019s no per-token cross-GPU sync.\n\n2) Throughput\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Single instance: You get the aggregate compute of 4 GPUs for one request stream. For a single large batch,",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its ",
