      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repository. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo and what the conventions should be.\n\nKey sections to cover:\n1. Where to put scripts, data, notes, patches, result summaries\n2. How to name run directories\n3. What to record for reproducibility\n4. How to decide whether a result is valid enough to promote\n\nLet me think about a sensible directory structure and conventions that would work for a real optimization repo. I'll make this practical and specific, not vague. I'll use a concrete example to ground it.\n\nLet me structure this as a proper technical guide with clear sections, code blocks for directory structures and examples, and actionable criteria. I'll aim for something that feels like it was written by someone who has actually maintained such a repo and hit the pain points.\n\nDirectory structure I'll propose:\n```\nbenchmarks/\n  <benchmark-name>/\n    scripts/\n    data/\n    notes/\n    patches/\n    results/\n      <run-dir>/\n        summary.md\n        raw/\n    README.md\n```\n\nRun directory naming: something like `YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS_<short-desc>` or with a hash. Let me think... For optimization work, you often want to encode the key variable (e.g., which patch, which config). Something like `20250115-143022_v2-cache-line-align` or with a short hash of the config.\n\nReproducibility: environment, compiler flags, hardware, input data checksums, exact commands, git commit of the repo, timing methodology.\n\nValidity criteria: statistical significance, warmup, multiple runs, no thermal throttling, consistent input, comparison against baseline, effect size vs noise floor.\n\nLet me write this as a clean, well-organized guide.\n</think>\n\n# Adding a New Benchmark to the Optimization Repo\n\n## 1. Directory Layout\n\nEvery benchmark lives under `benchmarks/<benchmark-name>/` using `kebab-case`. Create the full skeleton before writing any code:\n\n```\nbenchmarks/\n  <benchmark-name>/\n    README.md            # one-paragraph purpose, how to run, expected output\n    scripts/\n      run.sh             # single entry point; orchestrates everything\n      setup.sh           # fetches/builds inputs",
