      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific technical/operational task. They want at least 8 risks with: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and quality risks, not generic PM filler.\n\nLet me think about what actually goes wrong when you switch inference runtimes in production. I need to think like an ML engineer / SRE who has actually done this.\n\nReal risks when switching inference runtimes (e.g., from TensorFlow Serving to Triton, from TorchServe to a custom FastAPI+ONNX, from vLLM to TGI, from one GPU driver stack to another, from CPU to GPU, from one quantization format to another):\n\n1. **Numerical precision drift / output divergence** - Different runtimes compute the same model slightly differently due to floating-point ordering, kernel implementations, cuDNN vs cuBLAS paths, different reduction orders. This can cause subtle output changes that shift classification boundaries or degrade generation quality. Detection: A/B shadow traffic comparing outputs. Mitigation: Pre-deployment golden set comparison with tolerance thresholds. Rollback trigger: output divergence beyond threshold on shadow traffic.\n\n2. **Latency regression (p99/p95)** - The new runtime may have different batching behavior, different kernel launch overhead, different memory allocation patterns. p50 might be fine but p99 could be terrible due to different memory management, different CUDA stream usage, or different batching strategies. Detection: Load testing with production-like traffic patterns, monitoring p99/p95/p999. Mitigation: Pre-deployment load testing, setting SLOs. Rollback trigger: p99 exceeds SLO for X minutes.\n\n3. **Memory leak / GPU OOM under sustained load** - The new runtime may have different memory management. PyTorch's caching allocator vs TF's, or a new runtime that doesn't properly free intermediate tensors. Under sustained load, memory creeps up until OOM. Detection: GPU memory monitoring over time (not just at startup), memory profiling. Mitigation: Extended soak testing (24-48h), memory limits, monitoring. Rollback trigger: GPU memory growth rate exceeds threshold or OOM event.\n\n4. **Batching / concurrency behavior change** - Different runtimes handle concurrent requests differently. One might use dynamic batching, another static. The new runtime might serialize",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific technical/operational task. They want at least 8 risks with: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and qua",
